The top is represented by `count`, not by a special value in the array. A push stores at index `count` and then increases it. A pop decreases the count first, so the last stored value is returned.

The backing array doubles when full. This handles a stack larger than the starting capacity without using `Stack<int>` or another ready-made stack type.

```csharp
class ArrayStack
{
    private int[] values;
    private int count;

    public ArrayStack()
    {
        values = new int[4];
        count = 0;
    }

    public void push(int x)
    {
        if (count == values.Length)
        {
            int[] expanded = new int[values.Length * 2];
            for (int i = 0; i < values.Length; i++)
            {
                expanded[i] = values[i];
            }
            values = expanded;
        }

        values[count] = x;
        count++;
    }

    public int pop()
    {
        if (count == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        count--;
        int result = values[count];
        values[count] = 0;
        return result;
    }

    public int top()
    {
        if (count == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }
        return values[count - 1];
    }

    public bool isEmpty()
    {
        return count == 0;
    }
}
```

Pop, top, and the usual push operation take O(1) time. A full array resize takes O(n), so push is O(1) on average. Space complexity is O(n).

I used a plain `int[]` and grow it when there is no free position left. The stack does not need to move existing values during normal pushes. Only a resize copies the old values.

The empty case returns 0 for `pop()` and `top()`. That keeps the methods safe even though normal platform tests usually call them only when the stack has an element.

```java
class ArrayStack {
    private int[] values;
    private int size;

    public ArrayStack() {
        values = new int[4];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        if (size == values.length) {
            int[] bigger = new int[values.length * 2];
            for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
                bigger[i] = values[i];
            }
            values = bigger;
        }
        values[size++] = x;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (size == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        return values[--size];
    }

    public int top() {
        if (size == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        return values[size - 1];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

`push`, `pop`, `top`, and `isEmpty` are O(1) normally. A resize takes O(n), but the average cost of a push is O(1). The stored array needs O(n) space.

This uses a Go slice as the backing array and stores the current number of items separately. When the slice is full, a larger slice is copied over. The input `size` becomes the starting capacity, with a minimum of one.

An empty stack is handled by returning 0 from `Pop` and `Top`. The slice itself is changed as elements are pushed and popped, but no outside array is involved.

```go
type ArrayStack struct {
    values []int
    count  int
}

func NewArrayStack(size int) *ArrayStack {
    if size < 1 {
        size = 1
    }
    return &ArrayStack{
        values: make([]int, size),
    }
}

func (s *ArrayStack) Push(x int) {
    if s.count == len(s.values) {
        newValues := make([]int, len(s.values)*2)
        copy(newValues, s.values)
        s.values = newValues
    }
    s.values[s.count] = x
    s.count++
}

func (s *ArrayStack) Pop() int {
    if s.count == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    s.count--
    value := s.values[s.count]
    s.values[s.count] = 0
    return value
}

func (s *ArrayStack) Top() int {
    if s.count == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return s.values[s.count-1]
}

func (s *ArrayStack) IsEmpty() bool {
    return s.count == 0
}
```

Push, Pop, Top, and IsEmpty are O(1) on average. Growing the slice costs O(n) when it happens. The stack uses O(n) space.

A small fixed array is enough here. `topIndex` points to the last stored value, so pushing moves it forward and popping moves it back.

Dry run: after `push(4)` and `push(9)`, the top index is 1 and `top()` returns 9. Calling `pop()` returns 9 and changes the top index to 0.

```cpp
class ArrayStack {
private:
    int values[1000];
    int topIndex;

public:
    ArrayStack() {
        topIndex = -1;
    }

    void push(int x) {
        if (topIndex < 999) {
            values[++topIndex] = x;
        }
    }

    int pop() {
        if (topIndex == -1) {
            return 0;
        }
        return values[topIndex--];
    }

    int top() {
        if (topIndex == -1) {
            return 0;
        }
        return values[topIndex];
    }

    bool isEmpty() {
        return topIndex == -1;
    }
};
```

Each operation takes O(1) time. The array uses O(1) space because its capacity is fixed at 1000. Values pushed after the capacity is reached are ignored.

A Python list already behaves like a resizable array. I keep the newest value at the end, which matches the top of a stack, so `append` and `pop` give the needed order directly.

For a quick dry run, pushing 2, then 5, creates `[2, 5]`. The next pop removes 5, leaving `[2]`.

The code also checks the empty case instead of indexing an empty list.

```python
class ArrayStack:
    def __init__(self):
        self.values = []

    def push(self, x):
        self.values.append(x)

    def pop(self):
        if not self.values:
            return 0
        return self.values.pop()

    def top(self):
        if not self.values:
            return 0
        return self.values[-1]

    def isEmpty(self):
        return len(self.values) == 0
```

The usual time cost of every operation is O(1). Python may occasionally resize the list, but that does not change the average push cost. Space usage is O(n).

